`nxm import` migrates an existing Claude-host memory store into nexus-memory. Each fact is taken
in under its frontmatter `name:` as a stable key, so the migration is idempotent (a re-run upserts
in place) and non-destructive (the source files are never touched). `nxm init` surfaces the
suggestion; `nxm import` does the work.

`nxm init` detects the Claude memory dir for this project and proposes the import:

```console
$ nxm init
initialized memory at [CWD]/.nxs
  prefix:  ab12
  modules: memory
  agent files: created AGENTS.md
  hook:    wired SessionStart → `nxm prime || cat NEXUS_MEMORY.md 2>/dev/null`
  service: registered with the background service, which keeps this workspace's deadlines

You've set up memory. nxs also ships flow and chat — run `nxs init` to add.

Found 2 existing Claude memories — run `nxm import` to bring them into nexus-memory.

```

`nxm import` brings them in under their stable `name` keys; they are now first-class memories:

```console
$ nxm import
imported 2 memories from claude-memory

$ nxm memories
auth-jwt  auth uses JWT not sessions
race-flag  always run tests with the -race flag

```

A re-run is idempotent — the same names upsert in place, so nothing duplicates:

```console
$ nxm import
imported 2 memories from claude-memory

$ nxm memories
auth-jwt  auth uses JWT not sessions
race-flag  always run tests with the -race flag

```

The `--json` receipt names what came in and where from — `imported`, the `keys`, and the resolved
`source` — so an agent can report the migration without re-reading the store:

```console
$ nxm import --json
{"imported":2,"keys":["auth-jwt","race-flag"],"ok":true,"source":"claude-memory"}

```
